- The stair lands at the front, in a bright landing behind the cobogó (room for a desk); the hall runs back along the open stair to the primary suite.\P- Bath 2 sits between the two front bedrooms on the outer wall, with a window. Laundry upstairs, b
  3
eside the bedrooms.\P- Bed 3 becomes the den when the suite version is built downstairs.\P- Dimensions to the outside face of exterior walls and the centreline of partitions; areas the same way.\P- Ceilings 9'-6" on the ground floor, 9'-0" upstairs. 
  3
Doors 8'-0" high; window heads at 8'-6".\P- Stair: 17 risers at 7 7/16 in (10'-6" floor to floor), 16 treads at 10 1/8 in, 3'-3 3/4" clear. Open oak treads with a riser bar (4 in sphere rule); wall handrail at 34–38 in; cedar slats on the open side, 
  3
4 in max gaps.\P- Shared wall of a pair: 1-hour fire-resistance-rated, STC 50 or better, continuous to the roof deck, with no openings. Windows go on the front, back and outer side only; vent the dryer and the bath fans through the roof or the outer 
  3
wall.\P- Visitability (City of Austin, confirm at permit): a no-step entry, ground-floor doors with at least 30 in clear (the WC door 32 in), a ground-floor WC with a 30 × 48 in clear floor space, blocking for grab bars, switches at 48 in max and out
  3
lets at 15 in min.\P- Bedrooms have an egress window (5.7 sf clear, sill under 44 in). Smoke and CO alarms in each bedroom, the hall and each floor, interconnected.\P- Mechanical closet under the top of the stair: air handler and a heat-pump water he
  1
ater; louvred door, and duct the water heater if the closet has under 700 cu ft of air.
